@HostAddress = http://localhost:5125

### 0. Service info — lists the available endpoints
GET {{HostAddress}}/
Accept: application/json

### 1b. Brands — A WHOLE COLLECTION IN ONE CALL: mapper.Map<List<BrandDto>>(brands).
#     This endpoint used to read brands.Select(b => b.Map<BrandDto>(mapper)).ToList(), which is
#     the line every application ended up writing in every list endpoint. The destination SHAPE
#     is the type argument now, so BrandDto[], HashSet<BrandDto> and IReadOnlyList<BrandDto>
#     work the same way — and mapper.MapToBrandDtoList(brands) is the typed route that walks
#     no typeof chain at all.
#
#     FIRST, LOOK AT "aliases". Six of the eight seeded brands have a NULL Aliases column, and
#     every one of them comes back as [] rather than null. That is the NULL-COLLECTION POLICY,
#     and it is why BrandDto.Aliases can be declared non-nullable: no caller of this DTO ever
#     writes dto.Aliases?.Count, and the first place somebody would have forgotten to is a
#     NullReferenceException in production rather than a compile error. Request 1b2 asks the
#     database the same question and gets the same answer.
#
#     Then three things that were true before and still are. "isoCode": the entity spells it
#     ISOCode and the DTO spells it IsoCode, so this is case-insensitive matching working end
#     to end, through the database. "foundedYear": quoted, because the entity holds it as an
#     int and the DTO as a string — ShiftMapper converted it, invariantly, on its own.
#     "tags": the entity holds a List<string>, the DTO an IReadOnlyList<string>, and the DTO
#     gets its OWN copy rather than a read-only window onto the list EF is still tracking.
#
#     And "externalIds", which is the point of SM0010: the entity holds them as long and the
#     DTO declares them int, so any id past int.MaxValue maps to a different number — no
#     exception, nothing in the code to say so. That is why SM0010 is a build WARNING.
#     The seeded ids sit inside int range, because this same BrandDto is also PROJECTED —
#     directly in 1b2, and four levels down in 2 — and SQL REFUSES the overflow rather than
#     wrapping it: "Arithmetic overflow error converting expression to data type int". Worth
#     knowing on its own: the conversion that quietly returns the wrong number in C# is a hard
#     error in the database.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands
Accept: application/json

### 1b2. The SAME list, built by the DATABASE — db.Brands.ProjectTo<BrandDto>(mapper).
#     Run it straight after 1b and compare. Every brand comes back identical, "aliases"
#     included, which is the part worth checking rather than assuming: the two backends answer
#     that question in completely different code. In memory it is a ValueConverter builder that
#     substitutes an empty list; in SQL it is a coalesce spliced into the projection. Request
#     1b3 shows what that becomes.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/projected
Accept: application/json

### 1b3. The SQL behind 1b2 — and the one line to find in it:
#
#         COALESCE([b].[Aliases], CAST(N'[]' AS nvarchar(max)))
#
#     The null-collection policy is enforced BY THE DATABASE. EF read the column exactly as it
#     did before and applied the guard in the SELECT list, so the policy costs a COALESCE and
#     not a round trip.
#
#     WHY IT IS NOT ON EVERY COLLECTION, which is the interesting half. Look at [ExternalIds]:
#     it goes through OUTER APPLY OPENJSON with no coalesce anywhere near it. EF recognises a
#     primitive collection by the SHAPE of the expression around it, and `x ?? empty` is a shape
#     it cannot see through — guarding that one would have turned a working query into a
#     runtime "could not be translated", to protect against a null the type says cannot happen.
#     So the guard is written only where the source property is declared NULLABLE, which is the
#     developer's own statement about the column. Declare a collection nullable when it really
#     is, and both halves of ShiftMapper agree.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/projected?sql=true
Accept: text/plain

### 1b4. One brand — TWO doors in one endpoint.
#
#     First mapper.MapOrNull<BrandDto>(brand), for a row that may not be there. Plain Map would
#     THROW on a null source, and deliberately so: asking to build a DTO out of nothing is
#     almost always a bug, and one far cheaper to hear about at the mapping call than three
#     layers away. "The row was not found" is the exception — it is ordinary data — so
#     MapOrNull is the door for it, and it saves every such endpoint writing
#     `brand is null ? null : Map(brand)` by hand. See 1b5 for the null path.
#
#     Then the other overload, brand.Map(existingDto, mapper), which copies onto an object that
#     already exists. "updatedInPlace" proves it is the same instance, and
#     "injectedDependency" proves AppMapper really is a DI service with a dependency.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/1
Accept: application/json

### 1b5. A brand that is not there — MapOrNull hands back null and the endpoint returns 404,
#     with no exception thrown or caught anywhere along the way. That is the whole reason it
#     exists: the absence is a value the endpoint can test, rather than control flow.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/9999
Accept: application/json

### 1c. Stocks — the same generated maps, one object at a time, called DIRECTLY on the
#     mapper instance (mapper.Map<StockDto>(stock)) rather than through a collection overload
#     or an extension method. All three spellings run the same generated code; pick whichever
#     reads better where you are.
#     Look at "bayNumbers": the entity holds List<int>, the DTO IReadOnlyList<string>, so both
#     the collection shape and the element type were converted — and the POST below reads them
#     back the other way.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Accept: application/json

### 1c2. One stock — the instance form of the update overload, mapper.Map(stock, existing)
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks/1
Accept: application/json

### 1c3. Create a stock — the REVERSE map, StockDto -> Stock. Every request above goes
#     entity -> DTO; this one only compiles because AppMapper chains .ReverseMap().
#     The id below is ignored — the endpoint resets it so the database assigns the real one.
#     It is also the direction that has to READ the id back out of text: Stock.Id is an int
#     and StockDto.Id is a string, so the reverse map parses it. Leaving "id" out sends
#     empty text, which reads back as 0; send "id": "not-a-number" to watch it refuse.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Duhok Distribution Centre",
  "city": "Duhok",
  "code": "DHK-DC",
  "bayNumbers": ["1", "2", "3"]
}

### 1c4. Read back what 1c3 created (id 5 on a fresh database) — both directions of the
#     round trip come from that one CreateMap line.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks/5
Accept: application/json

### 1c5. Validation — a stock with no name returns 400 before any mapping happens
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/stocks
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "",
  "city": "Nowhere",
  "code": "NON-E"
}

### 1d. Products — PROJECTION, and the shortest demonstration of what that buys you.
#     ProductDto carries a BrandDto and a StockDto, and the endpoint contains no Include at
#     all. EF works the joins out from the map itself, so the response comes back three levels
#     deep from one query that reads only the columns the DTOs use.
#
#     Look inside for every earlier feature still working through the nesting: the brand's
#     "foundedYear" is a string over the entity's int, its "tags" an IReadOnlyList<string>
#     over a List<string>, the stock's "id" a string over an int, its "bayNumbers" strings
#     over ints. Add ?sql=true to see the query.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products
Accept: application/json

### 1e. DICTIONARIES — the collection shape that used to be a flat SM0002.
#
#     There is no database in this request, on purpose. A dictionary is how a KEYED PAYLOAD
#     arrives — a price per SKU, a note per line number — and that is where it is worth
#     showing, rather than inside a table it would need a value converter to live in.
#
#     One CreateMap<SupplierFeed, SupplierFeedDto>() covers all three properties below, and
#     each demonstrates a different half of the feature:
#
#       "prices"  the VALUES converted:  999.5 -> "999.5", invariant, so it is the same text in
#                 Baghdad and in Berlin. The dictionary is REBUILT to do it — generics are
#                 invariant, so a Dictionary<string, decimal> never becomes a
#                 Dictionary<string, string> however freely a decimal converts to text.
#       "notes"   the KEYS converted:    1 -> "1". This is the pair the build reports as
#                 SM0008, because converting keys is the one thing a dictionary can lose that a
#                 list cannot: two source keys can arrive as one, and the later wins. It cannot
#                 actually happen for int-to-string, and the note does not pretend otherwise
#                 — the rule is about converting keys, not about these two types. Narrow long
#                 keys onto int and the same note arrives as a real SM0010 warning.
#       "extras"  ABSENT from the request body, and {} in the response. The null-collection
#                 policy again, on a dictionary rather than on a list.
#
#     "ownsItsOwnData" is the other rule every collection follows: the DTO holds a NEW
#     dictionary, not a second reference to the request object's.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/supplier-feeds/preview
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "supplier": "Acme Distribution",
  "prices": {
    "APL-IP15P": 999.5,
    "SNY-PS5S": 499
  },
  "notes": {
    "1": "backordered",
    "2": "ships Monday"
  }
}

### 1e2. The same feed with EVERY dictionary left out. All three come back as {} rather than
#     null, so a caller of SupplierFeedDto never tests one for null. Send
#     "extras": null explicitly and the answer is the same — the policy is about the value,
#     not about whether the property was present in the JSON.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/supplier-feeds/preview
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "supplier": "Empty Feed"
}

### 1e3. A LIST of feeds — mapper.Map<List<SupplierFeedDto>>(feeds).
#     The collection overloads are generated for every declared map, not only for the ones with
#     a table behind them. Note the second feed sends no prices and the first sends no notes,
#     and both come back with {} for the one they left out.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/supplier-feeds/preview-many
Content-Type: application/json

[
  {
    "supplier": "Acme Distribution",
    "prices": { "APL-IP15P": 999.5 }
  },
  {
    "supplier": "Globex Supply",
    "notes": { "7": "arriving late" }
  }
]

### 1f. RECORDS — a destination that is not `new T { }`, which until now was SM0004.
#
#     ProductSummaryDto is a POSITIONAL RECORD, and BrandSummaryDto inside it is another. Neither
#     has a settable property: every value arrives as a constructor argument. ShiftMapper picks
#     the constructor and matches its parameters exactly as it matches members — by name, with
#     the same conversions, and with a nested map where a CreateMap exists:
#
#         return new ProductSummaryDto(
#             source.Id, source.Name, source.Sku,
#             ValueConverter.ToInvariantString(source.Price),   // decimal -> string
#             MapToBrandSummaryDto(source.Brand));              // a record inside a record
#
#     Note "price" comes back quoted and "isoCode" is filled from the entity's ISOCode — both
#     the conversion table and the case-insensitive fallback reach a constructor argument.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products/summary
Accept: application/json

### 1f2. AND IT PROJECTS, which is the reason records were worth doing rather than a convenience.
#
#         SELECT [p].[Id], [p].[Name], [p].[Sku], CONVERT(varchar(100), [p].[Price]),
#                [b].[Id], [b].[Name], [b].[ISOCode]
#         FROM [Products] AS [p]
#         INNER JOIN [Brands] AS [b] ON [p].[BrandId] = [b].[Id]
#
#     Compare it with request 1d, the same query into an ordinary class. Same joins, same
#     columns. The shape of the DTO changed and the query did not, which is the whole claim —
#     EF is handed one `new` with real arguments rather than an object initializer.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/products/summary?sql=true
Accept: text/plain

### 1g. REQUIRED MEMBERS — and both backends side by side so they can be compared.
#
#     `required` is a compile-time rule rather than a strong word: C# REFUSES an object
#     initializer that leaves one out. So an unmapped required member does not leave a property
#     empty, it stops the destination being built at all — and the build says which one:
#
#         warning SM0014: no Map method was generated to create 'InvoiceReceiptDto' because its
#                         required member 'LineCount' (string) is not mapped
#
#     Try it: delete either ForMember in AppMapper and watch the create half of that map
#     disappear with that message, instead of the build failing with a CS9035 inside a generated
#     file you cannot open.
#
#     "agree" is the interesting field. LineCount is required AND customized, and a customized
#     member is normally ABSENT from the generated projection — the expression lives in
#     AppMapper.cs and Compose splices it in at run time. A required one cannot be absent from a
#     template that is itself compiled, so the generator writes `LineCount = default!` and Compose
#     replaces it. If that ever stopped working these two objects would differ and nothing else
#     would look wrong. Request 1i is the same endpoint read for the OTHER thing it demonstrates.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1/receipt
Accept: application/json

### 1h. CONSTRUCTUSING — for the case convention cannot reach.
#
#     InvoiceLabelDto has no parameterless constructor and no settable Label, and no constructor
#     ShiftMapper could pick would know about the invoice-numbering service. So AppMapper supplies
#     the construction itself:
#
#         .ConstructUsing(s => new InvoiceLabelDto(_numbering.Prefix + s.Number))
#
#     It replaces CONSTRUCTION and nothing else — "customerName" below is still mapped by name,
#     by assignment, onto the object that expression returned. What cannot be assigned afterwards
#     is the expression's to fill, and the generated method's <remarks> lists exactly which.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1/label
Accept: application/json

### 1h2. AND IT CANNOT BE PROJECTED, which the build already said as an INFO:
#
#         info SM0015: the map from 'Invoice' to 'InvoiceLabelDto' builds its destination with
#                      ConstructUsing, so ProjectTo cannot use it; Map is unaffected
#
#     This request asks for the projection anyway. A projection reaches EF as one expression it
#     reads all the way down, and there is no general way to graft mapped properties onto an
#     object a delegate returned — so the generator emits a projection that THROWS, naming the
#     map and what to do instead, rather than no projection at all. (No projection would be a
#     CS0103 in the generated file the moment another map nested this one.)
#
#     The way out, when a map has to project, is the one request 1f uses: a constructor
#     ShiftMapper can match by name, plus ForMember for the arguments convention cannot work out.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1/label?project=true
Accept: text/plain

### 1i. MAPFROMSOURCE — letting the conversion table write the conversion.
#
#     "lineCount" is TEXT and Invoice.Lines.Count is an int. Plain MapFrom cannot express that:
#     its expression must return the DESTINATION member's type, so before this the only way was
#
#         .ForMember(d => d.Total, opt => opt.MapFrom(s => s.Lines.Sum(...).ToString("0.00")))
#
#     which is what the receipt's Total used to be. One call, and three losses: it hand-writes the
#     conversion this library exists to write, it takes the member out of the conversion table's
#     hands so SM0002/8/9/10 stop being reported for it, and ToString with no format provider
#     reads the machine's culture — so the total left a German server as "1596,00", in a library
#     whose ValueConverter exists to make exactly that impossible.
#
#     MapFromSource hands the value over instead:
#
#         LineCount = ValueConverter.ToInvariantString(...Value<..., int>("LineCount")(source))
#
#     and the projection gets the conversion as a lambda Compose splices onto the expression
#     rather than invoking, so EF still sees one expression. "agree" checks both halves match.
#
#     WATCH THE TWO TOTALS RENDER DIFFERENTLY: 1428.00 against 1428.0000, with agree still true.
#     Same decimal, different SCALE — EF writes CAST([Quantity] AS decimal(18,2)) * [UnitPrice],
#     so SQL multiplies scale 2 by scale 2 and gets 4 where C# gets 2. That is why Total is a
#     decimal and LineCount carries the demonstration: convert a computed decimal to text and the
#     scale it happens to have becomes the string.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/2/receipt
Accept: application/json

### 1j. CONDITION — the request that could not be written before it.
#
#     mapper.Map(patch, brand) assigns every mapped member, every time. JSON has no way to say
#     "absent", so this body arrives with Name = "", ISOCode = "" and FoundedYear = 0 — and
#     without a Condition the generated update writes all three over the tracked entity.
#
#     AppMapper guards each member on the incoming value:
#
#         .ForMember(d => d.Name, opt => opt.Condition((s, d, value) => !string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value)))
#
#     which generates, in the update overload:
#
#         {
#             var value = source.Name;
#             if (Customizations.Condition("Name", source, destination, destination.Name, value))
#                 destination.Name = value;
#         }
#
#     Think of it as a runtime opt.Ignore(): Ignore decides once at build time, Condition decides
#     per object. Nothing else about the member changed — it still matches by name, still goes
#     through the same conversion, still reports the same diagnostics.
#
#     The response returns "before", "after" and "sent" so the three fields that survived are
#     visible next to the blanks that would have overwritten them.
#
#     THE TRADE, which the build states rather than leaving you to find:
#
#         warning SM0017: the map from 'BrandPatch' to 'Brand' assigns 'Name', 'Country',
#                         'ISOCode', 'FoundedYear' behind a Condition, so ProjectTo cannot use
#                         it; Map is unaffected
#
#     A projection is one member initializer handed to the database, and there is no way to leave
#     a binding out per row. That is why a Condition belongs on a WRITE map like this one and not
#     on the read maps the list endpoints project.
PATCH {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "country": "Ireland"
}

### 1j2. Read brand 1 back — the name, ISO code and founded year are the ones 1j did not send,
#     and they are untouched.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/brands/1
Accept: application/json

### 2. Invoices — THE MAP AS A DATABASE QUERY.
#     db.Invoices.ProjectTo<InvoiceDto>(mapper), and again not one Include, yet the response
#     is four levels deep: invoice -> lines -> product -> brand and stock.
#
#     In the JSON: "total" is a number the entity never stores, "number" carries the "IQ/"
#     prefix from an injected service, and each line's "lineTotal" comes from the NESTED map's
#     own MapFrom — a customization two levels down that still translates.
#
#     ProjectTo returns an IQueryable, so the endpoint's OrderByDescending and Take(20) are
#     part of that same one query rather than filtering in memory afterwards.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Accept: application/json

### 2b. THE SQL ITSELF — the request to read carefully.
#
#         DECLARE @p int = 20;
#         DECLARE @_numbering_Prefix nvarchar(4000) = N'IQ/';
#
#         SELECT ... FROM (SELECT TOP(@p) ..., (
#             SELECT COALESCE(SUM(CAST([i0].[Quantity] AS decimal(18,2)) * [i0].[UnitPrice]), 0.0)
#             FROM [InvoiceLines] AS [i0] WHERE [i].[Id] = [i0].[InvoiceId]) AS [c],
#             @_numbering_Prefix + [i].[Number] AS [c0] FROM [Invoices] AS [i] ...) AS [i2]
#         LEFT JOIN (SELECT ..., CAST([i1].[Quantity] AS decimal(18,2)) * [i1].[UnitPrice] ...
#             FROM [InvoiceLines] AS [i1]
#             INNER JOIN [Products] AS [p] ON [i1].[ProductId] = [p].[Id]
#             INNER JOIN [Brands]   AS [b] ON [p].[BrandId]   = [b].[Id]
#             INNER JOIN [Stocks]   AS [s] ON [p].[StockId]   = [s].[Id] ...) AS [s0] ...
#
#     Four levels of joins nobody asked for — EF worked them out from the map, because the
#     whole nested graph reached it as ONE expression. Three things worth finding:
#
#       * the correlated subquery for Total, from a MapFrom on THIS map;
#       * Quantity * UnitPrice, from a MapFrom on the NESTED InvoiceLine map;
#       * @_numbering_Prefix — an injected service, read once in C# because it does not depend
#         on any row, and sent as a parameter named after the field it came from.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices?sql=true
Accept: text/plain

### 3. One invoice — THE SAME MAP, IN MEMORY. mapper.Map<InvoiceDto>(invoice).
#     One declaration in AppMapper feeds this and request 2 both, and they give the same
#     answer. What differs is who does the work: here the entity is loaded first and mapped in
#     C#, which is why every Include in this endpoint is load-bearing — drop the ThenInclude
#     for Brand and the DTO's brand arrives null, because there was nothing in memory to map.
#
#     This is the form for when you already have the entity: after a save, inside a unit of
#     work, or anywhere the object is in hand rather than in the database.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/1
Accept: application/json

### 3b. THE TWO RULES NESTING ADDS — both are edits rather than requests, both worth doing.
#
#     (a) A NESTED OBJECT WITH NO MAP IS A BUILD ERROR.
#
#     Comment out CreateMap<Product, ProductDto>() in AppMapper.cs and the build STOPS:
#
#         error SM0011: 'InvoiceLineDto.Product' needs a map from 'Product' to 'ProductDto'.
#                       Add CreateMap<Product, ProductDto>(), or
#                       CreateMap<ProductDto, Product>().ReverseMap(), or
#                       .Ignore(d => d.Product) to leave it unmapped on purpose.
#
#     Either direction counts — a pair registered by ReverseMap satisfies it exactly as well.
#     This is the only ERROR ShiftMapper reports: every other message describes a property
#     left unmapped, which may well be on purpose, while this one describes a property that
#     cannot be mapped YET, where a null in the response would look just like a null in the
#     database.
#
#     (b) A LOOP IS ALSO A BUILD ERROR.
#
#     There is no depth setting. Mapping goes as deep as the maps you declared and stops,
#     which is the whole rule. The one graph that cannot work that way is one that points back
#     at itself — give BrandDto a List<ProductDto> Products (ProductDto already has a BrandDto
#     Brand) and the build stops:
#
#         error SM0012: nested mapping never finishes — BrandDto.Products ->
#                       ProductDto.Brand -> BrandDto. Break the loop with
#                       .Ignore(d => d.Brand) on whichever side is the back-reference.
#
#     An error rather than a silent cut, because code that followed the loop would recurse
#     until the stack ran out — and in .NET that kills the process rather than failing one
#     request.
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Accept: application/json

### 4. Create an invoice
#    Only product id + quantity are sent; the server snapshots the current price.
#    The response is the created invoice with the full related graph.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Layla Ibrahim",
  "customerEmail": "layla.ibrahim@example.com",
  "lines": [
    { "productId": 2, "quantity": 1 },
    { "productId": 10, "quantity": 2 },
    { "productId": 23, "quantity": 4 }
  ]
}

### 5. Read back what request 4 created — it is mapped by the same CreateMap, so the
#     response has the full nested graph. Adjust the id to the one request 4 returned
#     (4 on a fresh database).
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/4
Accept: application/json

### 6. Not found — asking for an invoice that does not exist returns 404
GET {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices/9999
Accept: application/json

### 7. Validation — empty lines returns 400
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "No Lines",
  "customerEmail": "no.lines@example.com",
  "lines": []
}

### 8. Validation — unknown product id returns 400
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/invoices
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "customerName": "Bad Product",
  "customerEmail": "bad.product@example.com",
  "lines": [
    { "productId": 99999, "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}
